---
import SectionHeading from "../SectionHeading.astro";

// Mirrors src/main/services/scoring.ts in the app.
const byPrefix = [90, 68, 54, 42, 30, 18, 11, 7];
const slotWeights = Array.from({ length: 10 }, (_, i) => 1 / (1 + 0.15 * i));
// Illustrative review counts for a competitive keyword's top 10.
const reviews = [167_700, 122_300, 13_200, 56_500, 14_300, 4_900, 46_400, 148_000, 2_100, 9_800];
const strength = (n: number) => Math.min(1, Math.log10(n + 1) / 6);
---

<section class="relative border-y border-line bg-surface/40">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 py-24 sm:px-6 sm:py-32">
    <SectionHeading
      eyebrow="How the numbers work"
      title="No black box."
      tail="Every estimate, explained."
    >
      Apple only shares search volume through a Search Ads account, so OpenASO estimates it from
      public App Store data. Here's exactly how.
    </SectionHeading>

    <div class="mt-14 grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2" data-reveal-stagger="100">
      <article class="rounded-2xl border border-line bg-bg/70 p-6 sm:p-8" data-reveal>
        <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between">
          <h3 class="text-[17px] font-medium tracking-tight">Popularity</h3>
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px] text-dim">5–100</span>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          OpenASO finds the shortest prefix at which App Store autocomplete suggests the keyword.
          The fewer letters people need to type, the more often it's searched. Its place in the
          suggestion list counts too, and keywords Apple never suggests get 5, the same floor as
          Search Ads.
        </p>
        <div
          class="mt-8 space-y-2"
          aria-label="Score by letters typed before the keyword is suggested"
        >
          {byPrefix.map((score, i) => (
            <div class="grid grid-cols-[4.5rem_1fr_2rem] items-center gap-3 text-[12.5px]">
              <span class="font-mono text-dim">
                {i === byPrefix.length - 1 ? "8+ chars" : `${i + 1} char${i ? "s" : ""}`}
              </span>
              <div class="h-1.5 overflow-hidden rounded-full bg-white/[0.06]">
                <div
                  class="prefix-bar h-full rounded-full bg-gradient-to-r from-accent-from to-accent-to"
                  style={{ width: `${score}%`, "--i": i }}
                />
              </div>
              <span class="text-right font-mono text-fg tabular-nums">{score}</span>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="rounded-2xl border border-line bg-bg/70 p-6 sm:p-8" data-reveal>
        <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between">
          <h3 class="text-[17px] font-medium tracking-tight">Difficulty</h3>
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px] text-dim">1–100</span>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          How strong the current top 10 is: mostly their rating counts on a log scale, with the top
          spots weighted more, plus a little for each app with the exact keyword in its name. Few
          results make a keyword easier.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8">
          <div class="flex h-36 items-end gap-2" aria-hidden="true">
            {reviews.map((n, i) => (
              <div class="flex h-full flex-1 flex-col justify-end gap-1.5">
                <div
                  class="diff-bar relative overflow-hidden rounded-t-md bg-white/[0.06]"
                  style={{ height: `${slotWeights[i] * 100}%`, "--i": i }}
                >
                  <div
                    class="absolute inset-x-0 bottom-0 bg-gradient-to-t from-[#f97316] to-[#facc15]"
                    style={{ height: `${strength(n) * 100}%` }}
                  />
                </div>
                <span class="text-center font-mono text-[10.5px] text-dim">{i + 1}</span>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
          <p class="mt-4 text-[12.5px] text-dim">
            Bar height: how much each position counts. Fill: that app's rating count, log-scaled.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8 max-w-3xl text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-dim" data-reveal>
      Both are heuristics, so treat them as relative signals rather than Apple's own numbers. In our
      comparisons they track Astro's values closely for low- and mid-volume keywords. The code is
      open:{" "}
      <a
        href="https://github.com/iam-rohid/openaso/blob/main/src/main/services/scoring.ts"
        class="text-muted underline decoration-line-strong underline-offset-4 transition-colors hover:text-fg"
      >
        read scoring.ts
      </a>
      .
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  :global(.js) [data-reveal] .prefix-bar,
  :global(.js) [data-reveal] .diff-bar {
    transform: scale(0);
  }
  :global(.js) [data-reveal] .prefix-bar {
    transform: scaleX(0);
    transform-origin: left;
  }
  :global(.js) [data-reveal] .diff-bar {
    transform: scaleY(0);
    transform-origin: bottom;
  }
  :global(.js) [data-revealed] .prefix-bar,
  :global(.js) [data-revealed] .diff-bar {
    transition: transform 1.1s var(--ease-out-soft) calc(0.3s + var(--i) * 0.07s);
    transform: none;
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    :global(.js) [data-reveal] .prefix-bar,
    :global(.js) [data-reveal] .diff-bar {
      transition: none;
      transform: none;
    }
  }
</style>
